# E01 Reactive Architecture: parallel, 100 turns (the oracle bounds the turns, the example never stops); the explorers
# draw their moves from one generator in any order, and a message sent during a turn is read in that turn only if its
# receiver has not acted yet: how many exchanges fit in 100 turns depends on the scheduling, in the C# as in the C++.
# Compared: who starts, one first position per explorer (their values depend on the order of the draws), the
# vocabulary of the protocol (cover all), and its counts: no more pick-ups than samples found, no more unloads than
# pick-ups, at most the 10 samples, at least 50 moves (the C# makes about twice as many as the C++); and each move of
# an explorer (its positions in the order the planet receives them, first-in first-out per sender) goes one cell at
# most, horizontally or vertically, on the 11 x 11 grid.
cover all
fact start ^Starting (planet|explorer\d)$
fact position ^\t\[(explorer\d) -> planet\]: position (\d+) (\d+)$
fact change ^\t\[(explorer\d) -> planet\]: change (\d+) (\d+)$
fact carry ^\t\[(explorer\d) -> planet\]: carry (\d+) (\d+)$
fact pickup ^\t\[(explorer\d) -> planet\]: pick-up (res\d+)$
fact unload ^\t\[(explorer\d) -> planet\]: unload (res\d+)$
fact move ^\t\[planet -> (explorer\d)\]: move$
fact block ^\t\[planet -> (explorer\d)\]: block$
fact rock ^\t\[planet -> (explorer\d)\]: rock (res\d+)$
fact moved ^\t\[(explorer\d) -> planet\]: (?:position|change|carry) (\d+) (\d+)$
optional block
same start bag
same position count
check position count == 5
check position distinct(0) == 5
check pickup count <= rock.count
check unload count <= pickup.count
check move count >= 50
check pickup distinct(1) <= 10
check moved each by 0 abs($1 - prev$1) + abs($2 - prev$2) <= 1
check moved each $1 >= 0 and $1 <= 10 and $2 >= 0 and $2 <= 10
check change count > 0
check carry count <= change.count
